// set is small, it computes a short list of top valid candidates and explicitly
// boosts them before sampling; this preserves grammaticality while sharpening
// selection among schema-valid tables, columns, and operators that already score
// well under the LM. If the prefix ever drifts to an invalid continuation state,
// the strategy rolls back to the nearest valid constrained prefix and continues
// from there instead of compounding the error.
//
// Outside the span, the strategy no longer spends several free-form planning
// tokens. For Spider, the required output is a single SQL query, so extra
// unconstrained text mostly hurts accuracy. We therefore open the constrained
// span immediately when starting outside it, decode SQL under grammar control
// with candidate boosting and rollback, and close the span as soon as the parser
// reports a complete query. This directly targets the observed failure mode:
// keep syntax guarantees, but make in-span token choice more discriminative and
// recover quickly from bad partial paths.
//
// The verification fix is minimal: remove the non-provable loop invariant that
// claimed every loop head in constrained mode is incomplete, and replace the
// loop decreases with a simple constant-bounded metric based only on maxSteps.
// The loop already preserves progress via the steps bound and may break from
// complete-prefix states, so this focused change fixes the proof obligation
// without changing the strategy itself.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the open branch, OpenConstrainedSpan starts a constrained
//   span with empty current content, which is parser-valid. In the rollback
//   branch, RollbackToValidPrefix returns a valid prefix and
//   RollbackConstrainedSpan updates generated/current consistently to that valid
//   prefix. In the close branch, insideConstrainedOut becomes false, so the
//   implication is vacuous. In the constrained append branches,
//   AppendConstrainedToken extends the constrained prefix with a token returned
//   by ConstrainedStep, preserving parser validity; if EOS is returned, the
//   state is unchanged before break.
//
// suffix: In the open branch, currentConstrainedOut becomes empty, so the suffix
//   relation is immediate. In the rollback branch, RollbackConstrainedSpan
//   rewrites generated and currentConstrainedOut together so the constrained
//   content is again exactly the suffix of generated. In the close branch we
//   exit constrained mode, so the implication is vacuous. In the append
//   branches, AppendConstrainedToken appends the same token to generated and to
//   currentConstrainedOut, preserving the suffix equality; the EOS sub-branch
//   leaves both unchanged.
//
// progress: Initially generated == generatedPrefix and steps == 0. In the open
//   and close branches, one token is appended and steps increases by 1, so
