Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy opened the constrained span too eagerly and then mixed
// unconstrained model preferences with only weak parser filtering. On Spider,
// that often yields grammar-valid but semantically bad SQL prefixes such as
// degenerate identifier runs ("SELECT <id> <id> ,"). To address the observed
// failures, this revision makes two major changes.
//
// First, outside the constrained region we no longer wait for a fuzzy SQL-like
// signal. We open the constrained span immediately on the first decoding step
// whenever budget remains. This matches the task requirement that the output is
// a single SQL query and avoids wasting tokens on preamble or drifting before
// SQL begins.
//
// Second, once inside the span we use a stricter parser-aware policy. If the
// current constrained prefix is complete, we close immediately. Otherwise, we
// query the parser for whether the search space is narrow. In narrow states we
// rely on ConstrainedStep directly, letting the parser dominate token choice.
// In wider states we still inspect model logits, but only over parser-valid
// candidates returned by TopValidCandidates, and we append the highest-logit
// valid candidate from that filtered set. This removes the previous failure
// mode where an unconstrained argmax token was accepted whenever locally valid
// even if it led toward poor repetitive identifier continuations.
//
// The result is an always-delimited single constrained SQL span, with every
// token inside grammar/schema checked, earlier closure on complete queries, and
// less opportunity for unconstrained drift or repetition.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
