Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Spider text-to-SQL often has a long unconstrained lead-in (question/schema
// context already in the prompt) followed by a single SQL query region that
// benefits from grammar-and-schema validation. This strategy tracks three
// pieces of state: the full generated output (`generated`), whether we are
// currently inside a constrained SQL span (`insideConstrainedOut`), and the
// currently generated SQL fragment inside that span (`currentConstrainedOut`).
// Outside the span, we use chunked unconstrained decoding to move quickly until
// EOS or an opening delimiter token `<<` appears. Inside the span, we first
// check whether the current SQL prefix is already complete and, if so, close
// the span immediately. Otherwise we use a speculative fast path: look at the
// model argmax under the constrained prompt and append it directly when it is a
// parser-valid next SQL token; if not, fall back to `ConstrainedStep`, which
// consults the parser and schema-aware grammar to choose a valid continuation.
// This tracked state supports valid generation because the constrained suffix is
// always exactly the parser-validated SQL fragment currently being built, and
// all transitions into, within, and out of the constrained region update the
// suffix/state pair in lockstep.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the chunked unconstrained branch, we either remain
//   outside the span (implication vacuous) or `stoppedOpen` becomes true, in
